[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Now the last big fork before I can present a design for piece #1.

  Multi-user requires knowing who's logged in, and auth is the part that's annoying to swap later because sessions, user
  identity, and every protected endpoint get built on top of it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether you own the credentials or delegate them.

  - [1mSession cookie + hashed password[0m — fully self-contained, no external accounts, no network dependency, easy to test
    offline. Costs you the boring surface area: hashing, login/logout, and eventually password reset. Well-trodden in
    FastAPI.
  - [1mOAuth ("sign in with Google")[0m — no passwords to store or leak, and users are already signed in. Costs provider
    setup, client secrets in config, and a dependency on an external service during local dev and tests.
  - [1mDev stub now, real auth later[0m — a hardcoded user switcher. Gets us to notifications fastest. Genuinely risky: "add
    real auth later" tends to mean auditing every endpoint you wrote in the meantime. Defensible only if this never
    leaves your machine.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m session cookie + hashed password. For a small team it's the least total work, it keeps tests
  hermetic with no external services, and it gives piece #3 a real [38;5;153muser_id[39m to attach subscriptions and notifications to
  — which is exactly what the notification system needs.
